,title,link,text,language,family
0,Kwakwakaʼwakw music - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kwakwakaʼwakw_music,The Kwakwakaʼwakw peoples have practiced sacred and secular music for thousands of years.,Kwak’wala,Wakashan
1,Kwakʼwala - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kwakʼwala,"Kwakʼwala has complex word formation, featuring a polysynthetic morphology that is almost exclusively suffixing like other Wakashan languages. Its verbs feature suffixes for mood, aspect, and person, with multiple of these meanings often existing in a single suffix.",Kwak’wala,Wakashan
2,"Sounds of Kwak'wala - Articulation - YouTubeGuide to the Indigenous Materials at the American ...Mourning | Mourning | Education | Living Tradition, The ...Kwakwaka’wakw songs preserve ancient traditions - Vancouver Sun",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fTsQTYpCAXw,"Nov 20, 2020 · Enjoy the videos and music you love, upload original content, and share it all with friends, family, and the world on YouTube. The materials in this section are all comparative vocabularies and analyses of mutiple Wakashan languages, primarily Nuu-chah-nulth, Kwak'wala, and Ditidaht. Distinction between these languages will be more apparent in the materials in themselves. This is the Kwa k´ wala-speaking peoples' healthy process of letting go of the deceased. Grieving and releasing their tears and sorrow for their loss which allows them to continue on with their own life. Apr 26, 2014 · What’s challenging for everyone is that he’s teaching them to sing in Kwak’wala, the language spoken by the Kwakwaka’wakw of Alert Bay and northern Vancouver Island. Funded by the park... What is Kwakwaka'wakw music? ' Kwakwaka'wakw music is a sacred and ancient art of the Kwakwaka'wakw peoples that has been practiced for thousands of years. Where did the Wakashan language come from? Northern Wakashan (or Kwakiutlan) speakers likely expanded outward from the north of Vancouver Island, displacing Salishan languages on the mainland of what is now British Columbia. Kwakʼwala was first written by missionaries during the colonization of the Pacific Northwest. Who are the Kwakwaka'wakw? The Kwakwaka'wakw are a collective of twenty-five nations : 12–13 of the Wakashan language family who altogether form part of a larger identity comprising the Indigenous Peoples of the Pacific Northwest Coast, located in what is known today as British Columbia, Canada. What are the three types of words in Kwakwala? Kwakʼwala formally distinguishes only three classes of words: predicates/substantives, particles, and exclamatory forms. Nouns and verbs are distinguished mainly by syntactic context. Thus, the bare form kʼʷasʼ ""sit"" is a verb; combined with an article-like particle, it serves as a noun: jəχa kʼʷasʼ ""the one who sits"" (Boas 1947).",Kwak’wala,Wakashan
3,"Kwak̓wala hymns with Sarah Nielson and Andrew Speck - YouTubeSounds of Kwak'wala - Articulation - YouTubeGuide to the Indigenous Materials at the American ...Mourning | Mourning | Education | Living Tradition, The ...Kwakwaka’wakw songs preserve ancient traditions - Vancouver Sun",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ijRqo9hsrPI,"Feb 14, 2025 · Enjoy the videos and music you love, upload original content, and share it all with friends, family, and the world on YouTube. Nov 20, 2020 · Enjoy the videos and music you love, upload original content, and share it all with friends, family, and the world on YouTube. The materials in this section are all comparative vocabularies and analyses of mutiple Wakashan languages, primarily Nuu-chah-nulth, Kwak'wala, and Ditidaht. Distinction between these languages will be more apparent in the materials in themselves. This is the Kwa k´ wala-speaking peoples' healthy process of letting go of the deceased. Grieving and releasing their tears and sorrow for their loss which allows them to continue on with their own life. Apr 26, 2014 · What’s challenging for everyone is that he’s teaching them to sing in Kwak’wala, the language spoken by the Kwakwaka’wakw of Alert Bay and northern Vancouver Island. Funded by the park... What is Kwakwaka'wakw music? ' Kwakwaka'wakw music is a sacred and ancient art of the Kwakwaka'wakw peoples that has been practiced for thousands of years. Where did the Wakashan language come from? Northern Wakashan (or Kwakiutlan) speakers likely expanded outward from the north of Vancouver Island, displacing Salishan languages on the mainland of what is now British Columbia. Kwakʼwala was first written by missionaries during the colonization of the Pacific Northwest. Who are the Kwakwaka'wakw? The Kwakwaka'wakw are a collective of twenty-five nations : 12–13 of the Wakashan language family who altogether form part of a larger identity comprising the Indigenous Peoples of the Pacific Northwest Coast, located in what is known today as British Columbia, Canada. What are the three types of words in Kwakwala? Kwakʼwala formally distinguishes only three classes of words: predicates/substantives, particles, and exclamatory forms. Nouns and verbs are distinguished mainly by syntactic context. Thus, the bare form kʼʷasʼ ""sit"" is a verb; combined with an article-like particle, it serves as a noun: jəχa kʼʷasʼ ""the one who sits"" (Boas 1947).",Kwak’wala,Wakashan
4,Kwakwaka’wakw songs preserve ancient traditions - Vancouver Sun,https://vancouversun.com/news/metro/kwakwakawakw-songs-preserve-ancient-traditions,"Apr 26, 2014 · What’s challenging for everyone is that he’s teaching them to sing in Kwak’wala, the language spoken by the Kwakwaka’wakw of Alert Bay and northern Vancouver Island. Funded by the park...",Kwak’wala,Wakashan
5,Kwakwakaʼwakw music - Wikiwand,https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Kwakwakaʼwakw_music,"The Kwak'wala word for ""summer song"" is baquyala, and the word for ""winter song"" is ts’ē⁾k·ala.[4]: 75 The arrival of the next winter season is celebrated each year in a four-day festival of song and dance called tsetseqa, or Winter Ceremonial.[5]...",Kwak’wala,Wakashan
6,Wakashan – Kwak’wala Keyboards – Wiga’xa n’s yak a nt alape,https://yakantalape.ca/wakashan-kwakwala-keyboards/,Wakashan – Kwak’wala Keyboards. Languagegeek provides free fonts and free keyboard layouts which try to cover all of the characters (alphabetical letters/syllabics) necessary for writing Native languages. General information about typography and orthographies is also available.,Kwak’wala,Wakashan
7,PhrasalProminenceKwakwala_CLA2024_abstract,https://cla-acl.ca/pdfs/resumes-2024-abstracts/Elfner.pdf,"Kwak’wala Stress as Phrasal Prominence Emily Elfner (York University). Kwak’wala (Wakashan: British Columbia) shows several unusual prosodic features including a typologically rare default-to-opposite stress system (Boas 1947; Grubb 1969; Shaw 2009), and extensive mismatches...",Kwak’wala,Wakashan
8,"Kwakwakawakw | Ubicacion, Lengua, Vestimenta... - Pueblos Indigenas",https://pueblosindigenas.es/de-canada/kwakwakawakw/,Wakashan (Northern Wakashan). Lengua. Kwak’wala; ISO 639-3: kwk. Población estimada. ~5.500 personas (Censo Canadá 2021); ~600 hablantes.,Kwak’wala,Wakashan
9,Guide to the Indigenous Materials at the American ...,https://indigenousguide.amphilsoc.org/entry/39974,"The materials in this section are all comparative vocabularies and analyses of mutiple Wakashan languages, primarily Nuu-chah-nulth, Kwak'wala, and Ditidaht. Distinction between these languages will be more apparent in the materials in themselves.",Kwak’wala,Wakashan
